WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.080
Hi, I'm Dan from BBC Learning English

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.840
and today I'm going to tell you something about

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.940
collective nouns.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.380
Collective nouns are often called group nouns.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.340
That is because they represent a number of

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.100
things together.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:13.980
Examples are:

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.760
government, family and team.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:17.980
So, what's the big deal?

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.000
The special thing about these collective nouns

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.220
is that they can be singular or plural.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.320
If you consider your family to be a group of individuals,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.980
then the pronoun is they and the verb is plural.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.980
My family are happy.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.700
But your family can also be considered as a single unit,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.520
a machine that all works together.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.400
In this case, the pronoun is it and the verb is singular.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.760
My family is happy.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.060
This does not apply to all collective nouns,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.180
so be careful.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.760
Police and staff are always plural.

